messages:
Sam: Heads up — running our Android instrumentation tests with MockWebServer started failing intermittently on CI with SSL handshake / socket closed errors. Local runs are flaky too. Anyone seen MockWebServer + certificate pinning + Retrofit/OkHttp behaving weirdly?

Sam: Error looks like this in logs: 
```
javax.net.ssl.SSLHandshakeException: Unexpected TLS packet: 0x15
Caused by: java.io.EOFException: SSL peer closed connection during handshake
```

Jiwoo: Is the MockWebServer set up with TLS using handshakes from a custom trust manager or self-signed cert? CI could be hitting an old JVM security provider.

Sam: We use MockWebServer#useHttps(sslSocketFactory, false) with a test cert we generate in the runner. Pinning is enabled via OkHttp certificate pinner in the app.

Mike: Two quick things to try: 1) ensure MockWebServer and OkHttp have the same TLS protocols enabled (eg TLSv1.2). 2) try disabling certificate pinning just to confirm it's the pin failing vs handshake.

Sam: Disabled pinning locally — handshake still errors sometimes. Also seeing a lot of "Connection closed prematurely" traces and the tests retry which sometimes replays POSTs. Suspect connection pool or dispatcher reuse is clobbering test server.

Jiwoo: Replaying POSTs sounds like OkHttp retry on connection failure + non-repeatable request body. Are you using streaming request bodies (e.g., RequestBody.create(stream))? Retrofit multipart?

Sam: It's simple JSON POST using a RequestBody created from a Buffer via Gson. Not streaming. But our app-level interceptor does this to add auth header and maybe rewrites body for analytics: 
```
@Override public Response intercept(Chain chain) throws IOException {
  Request req = chain.request();
  // add header
  Request.Builder b = req.newBuilder().header("x-cid","test");
  // sometimes reads body bytes for logging
  if (req.body() != null) {
    Buffer buf = new Buffer();
    req.body().writeTo(buf); // <- could exhaust body if not buffered
    log.debug(buf.readUtf8());
  }
  return chain.proceed(b.build());
}
```

Mike: Ahhhh reading the body then proceeding with the original request can make the body non-repeatable for retries. If OkHttp fails and tries to retry, the body stream has been consumed. Solution: buffer the body into a byte[] and create a new RequestBody from it, or only log bodies for idempotent methods.

Sam: Good catch. But why does this only fail on CI / instrumented device intermittently and not in plain unit tests?

Jiwoo: Two reasons: 1) CI network conditions cause occasional connection drops triggering retries. 2) Android's OkHttp builds sometimes have different default connection pool and keepalive timings across API levels leading to stale sockets being detected and closed mid-handshake. Combined with an exhausted request body you get EOF/handshake errors.

Sam: Makes sense. We're also using MockWebServer with HTTPS — could the server be closing TLS early if it sees malformed ClientHello due to a partial request?

Mike: If the client reuses a stale socket that's been closed by the server, the client-side TLS handshake will fail with EOF or TLS packet errors. OkHttp by default validates socket health before reuse but under racey timing it can slip. Increasing connectTimeout or configuring pingInterval/keepAlive might hide the symptom but patching the interceptor is the real fix.

Sam: Plan: stop reading request.body() directly in interceptor, always wrap with a buffered byte[] copy if we must log. Also set OkHttpClient.retryOnConnectionFailure(true) is okay, but we'll guard to not retry non-idempotent requests. Any tips on guarding retries for POSTs?

Jiwoo: OkHttp doesn't have an API to disable retries per-method, but you can set retryOnConnectionFailure(false) on client and implement a custom Call.Factory that wraps calls and only retries idempotent methods. Simpler: in interceptor detect method != GET && != HEAD && != PUT?then set header "x-no-sdk-retry" and use an EventListener or ConnectionPool filter? bit messy.

Mike: Another pragmatic approach: create a wrapper RequestBody that is repeatable by buffering the bytes at creation time. Then retries are safe even for POST. Most SDKs do this for small JSON payloads.

Sam: For immediate CI unblock, I'll patch interceptor to buffer body for logging and add a small backoff for flaky tests. I'll also add a MockWebServer TLS config check to force TLSv1.2 only.

ci-bot: build #2879 (instrumentation) failed: flaky-network. Retrying pipeline. :gear:

Sam: Thanks — I'll send a PR with changes and a reproducible androidTest that toggles certificate pinning and asserts no duplicate POSTs (server request count stays 1).

Jiwoo: Nice. Also add a comment in the devex docs about 'Interceptor side-effects: don't consume request bodies' — this has bitten teams before.

Mike: If you want, I can paste a small RepeatableRequestBody helper later.

Sam: Please do — would be helpful for the PR. :thumbsup:
